    "content": "T. Rowe Price faced a major class-action lawsuit in 2017 alleging ERISA violations, resulting in a $7 million settlement in 2021. The lawsuit claimed the company packed its employee 401(k) plans with high-fee, in-house funds instead of better-performing, cheaper alternatives. Recent allegations also include a 2026 suit regarding failure to prevent account takeover fraud. Key Details of the $7M 401(k) Settlement (2017-2022): Self-Dealing Allegations: Plaintiffs claimed T. Rowe Price favored its own investment products over better, cheaper options to maximize fees. Damages: The suit estimated participants paid roughly $27 million more in fees than necessary and missed out on over $123 million in potential retirement savings. Resolution: The settlement involved paying $7 million and adding a brokerage window to allow more investment options. Other Incidents: 2026 Cybersecurity Suit: A lawsuit filed in February 2026 alleges the firm failed to protect a client from a significant \"account takeover\" fraud, resulting in a >$700k loss. 2016 Voting Error: T. Rowe Price agreed to pay up to $194 million to compensate clients due to a voting error related to a Dell LBO. Ongoing Fiduciary Scrutiny:The 2026 lawsuit underscores a continuing focus on how financial institutions manage client assets, including the use of \"red flag\" alerts for potential fraud.",
